Ovarian Cancer
Haematology & Oncology
Persistent pelvic/abdominal pain, Increasing abdominal girth/ascites, Early satiety, Complex adnexal mass on ultrasound

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: HMO-036
Reason for Visit: Acute exacerbation of severe, unremitting pelvic pain and rapid abdominal distension causing significant respiratory compromise at rest.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a patient experiencing a rapid, massive increase in abdominal girth over the past 48 hours, now resulting in severe shortness of breath when lying flat and agonizing pelvic pain. Urgent in-person evaluation was necessary to evaluate for tense malignant ascites, impending bowel obstruction, or ovarian torsion, which could not wait until the next available daytime clinic appointment without risking severe respiratory failure.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My stomach has blown up so tight I cannot breathe when I lie down, and there is a constant, tearing pain deep in my pelvis."
History of Present Illness: The 62-year-old patient reports a 3-month insidious history of vague, persistent, and unremitting lower abdominal pain initially dismissed as indigestion. They note profound early satiety, stating they can only eat a few bites before feeling completely full, and a 5 kg unintentional weight loss despite their increasing abdominal girth. Over the past 48 hours, the abdominal bloating escalated rapidly, causing acute orthopnoea and a severe exacerbation of the deep pelvic pain. They also note new-onset urinary frequency over the past week.
Functional Status: Confined to a recliner chair; entirely unable to lie flat in bed due to breathlessness.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; high risk of a fall due to altered center of gravity and severe fatigue.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 | HR: 108 | Temp: 37.1 | RR: 24 | SpO2: 94% | Wt: 72 Kg (rapidly fluctuating due to fluid)
General: Cachectic adult with a massively protuberant abdomen. Breathing is shallow and rapid.
Physical Exam:
Abdomen: Tense, shiny, and massively distended. Shifting dullness is strongly positive, confirming large-volume ascites. Deep palpation is limited by fluid, but a firm, irregular mass is palpable in the left lower pelvic quadrant. A firm, palpable umbilical nodule (Sister Mary Joseph nodule) is noted.
Respiratory: Decreased air entry at both lung bases secondary to diaphragmatic splinting from the massive ascitic fluid volume. No wheeze or crackles.
Cardiovascular: Tachycardic, regular rhythm. Capillary refill 3 seconds.
Extremities: 1+ bilateral pitting oedema to the mid-calves.
Environmental Observations: Multiple pillows stacked on the bed.
Medication Reconciliation: Taking over-the-counter antacids and paracetamol with absolute zero efficacy.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Ovarian Cancer - Status: Exacerbated (Tense Malignant Ascites)
Differentials:

End-Stage Liver Cirrhosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: the absence of classical stigmata (spider naevi, jaundice, palmar erythema) and the presence of a distinct, firm pelvic mass and an umbilical metastatic nodule.

Irritable Bowel Syndrome (IBS) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: the massive clinical ascites, orthopnoea, significant cachexia, and advanced age of onset, which unequivocally point to an invasive malignant process rather than functional bloating.

Congestive Heart Failure - Ruled out/unlikely due to: the normal JVP, lack of an S3 gallop, and the ascites being massively out of proportion to the mild peripheral oedema.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with the classic symptom complex for advanced ovarian malignancy: vague pelvic pain, early satiety, and rapidly increasing abdominal girth. The acute presentation of orthopnoea and tachycardia is driven by tense malignant ascites severely splinting the diaphragm and compromising venous return. Delaying this assessment would expose the patient to progressive respiratory failure, unbearable pelvic pain, and potential bowel ischemia from intra-abdominal pressure. The urgent home visit was essential to physically identify the respiratory compromise, rule out an acute surgical abdomen, and arrange immediate hospital transfer for therapeutic paracentesis and definitive imaging.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Immediate referral and ambulance transfer to the Emergency Department for urgent therapeutic paracentesis (to relieve respiratory distress) and a transvaginal ultrasound/CT abdomen (anticipating a complex, solid and cystic adnexal mass with irregular septations). Handover included the requirement for a baseline serum CA-125 level.
Positive Behavioral Change:

Remain seated strictly upright at a 45-degree angle or higher. By utilizing gravity, you pull the massive fluid collection downward, relieving the immediate mechanical pressure on your lungs and making breathing easier.

Consume strictly zero fluids or solid food tonight. By keeping your stomach entirely empty, you avoid adding further volume to your tightly stretched abdomen and ensure your stomach is safe for any emergency procedures at the hospital.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Immediate ED transfer overrides routine GP follow-up.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

